  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a loosely- \norganized cluster of showers and thunderstorms moving east into \nportions of Northwest and North-Central Ohio this afternoon. \nFor the most part, peak wind gusts within this cluster are \ngenerally below 50 mph and non-severe, supported by weak MLCAPE\naround 1000 J/kg and gradually warming cloud tops, though \nisolated severe wind gusts of 55 to 60 mph will remain possible \ninto this evening given the strong shear in place, especially \nacross the Severe Thunderstorm Watch area which is in place\nuntil 8 PM. Otherwise, main concern will be the moderate to \nheavy rain associated with the stratiform convection approaching\nthe area, with radar estimating rates in excess of 2 inches per\nhour which could result in minor/nuisance flooding given the \nalready- saturated ground across much of the area. A Flood Watch\nremains in effect into Wednesday morning. Showers and storms \nshould exit east of the area by around 10/11 PM, with some \npatches of fog possible overnight.\n\nThe next chance for showers and thunderstorms appears to be\nWednesday night into Thursday as a weak trough slides south\nacross the area. Not anticipating anything severe, though could\nsee a few stronger storms with small hail or wind gusts\napproaching 50 mph, especially across Northeast Ohio. This\ntrough is expected to stall near or just south of the US-30\ncorridor Thursday afternoon or evening. Another wave of low\npressure will move east along this boundary Thursday night, with\nheavy rain being the primary concern. There remains some\nuncertainty with the boundary placement (could be further south)\nand therefore the flooding threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe next period for unsettled weather will be Saturday night\ninto Sunday as a low pressure system moves east through the\nGreat Lakes, extending a cold front across the area Sunday\nnight. Will continue to monitor the threat for any stronger\nstorms, especially Sunday afternoon with the latest guidance \nsuggesting a potent 700 mb shortwave of 30 to 35 knots entering\nthe area.",
